Steps to Add Repo with SSH:
Here is exactly where you need to place both keys to make the connection work:

1. The Public Key goes into Bitbucket

The public key acts as the lock. You must add it to your Bitbucket repository so Bitbucket knows your cluster is allowed to read the code.
1. Open your public key file (e.g., id_ed25519.pub) and copy the entire text string.
2. Log into Bitbucket and navigate to your specific repository.
3. On the left-hand menu, click Repository settings.
4. Scroll down and click on Access keys (under the Security or Settings section).
5. Click Add key, paste your public key string into the box, give it a name (like argocd-stage), and click Save.

2. The Private Key goes into the Argo CD UI

The private key acts as the digital signature. You must give it to Argo CD so it can authenticate itself against Bitbucket.
1. Open your private key file (e.g., id_ed25519) and copy the entire block of text—including the -----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY----- and -----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY----- lines.
2. Log into your Argo CD GUI.
3. Go to Settings (the gear icon on the left menu) → Repositories → Click Connect Repo.
4. Fill out the connection settings like this:
	• Choose connection method: Select VIA SSH
	• Repository URL: Paste your Bitbucket SSH URL (e.g., git@bitbucket.org:your-workspace/your-repo.git)
	• SSH Private Key: Paste your entire private key text block directly into this box.
	• TLS Options: Check the box for Skip server verification.
5. Click the Connect button at the top.
Once you hit connect, does the repository status change to a green SUCCESSFUL status, or are you hitting a validation error when pasting the key?

command to generate key:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "argocd-bitbucket-key" -f ./argocd-bitbucket
